---
title: Bacon-Maple Walnut Pie and Malt Frozen Custard
description: Even if you like your morning bacon limp, fry it crisp enough that you can shard it into pieces. These will soften in the pie, providing a distinctly porky flavor to go with the walnuts and maple syrup. You don’t want to burn the bacon—doing so can add unpleasantly bitter notes. But get it crisp enough to stand up to the long baking time and still release lots of sophisticated, caramelized flavor without ending up as chewy, rubbery bits.
image: https://foodschmooze.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/08/Bacon-Maple-Walnut-Pie-with-Malt-Frozen-Custard_Eric-Medsker_slider.jpg
servings: 1
yield: 1 9-inch pie
author: Robyn Doyon-Aitken
source: https://foodschmooze.org/recipe/bacon-maple-walnut-pie-malt-frozen-custard
course: Dessert
cuisine: American
---

== Crust ==

Fry @bacon slices{4%oz}, turning occasionally with a fork, in a #skillet{} set over medium heat, until crisp, ~{3-4%minutes}. Transfer the bacon to a paper towel–lined plate and set aside.

Reserve @bacon fat{2%tbsp} from the skillet, then measure the remainder of the fat in the skillet into a heat-safe liquid measuring cup. Cool for ~{10%minutes} at room temperature. Add enough @solid vegetable shortening{up to 5%tbsp} so that the total volume in the cup measures @fat{6%tbsp}.

Pour @all-purpose flour{1%cup} into a large bowl and add the combined fat in the measuring cup. Using a pastry cutter or a fork, work the fat through the flour until the mixture resembles coarse cornmeal.

Stir @water{2%tbsp} and @apple cider vinegar{1/2%tsp} in a small cup or bowl. Add to the flour mixture and stir until a soft, pliable, but not sticky dough forms, adding more cold water in 1-teaspoon increments as necessary. Gather the dough into a ball, dust it with @flour{}(additional), and roll it into an 11-inch crust. Center and set the crust into a 9-inch pie plate. Trim the edges and flute them as desired.

== Filling ==

Beat @eggs{3%large} (at room temperature), @maple syrup{1%cup}, @granulated white sugar{1%cup}, @dark brown sugar{1/2%cup}, and @pure vanilla extract{1%tbsp} in a large bowl with an electric mixer at medium speed until smooth and uniform, even a little foamy at the top. Beat in the reserved @bacon fat{2%tbsp}.

Working by hand, crumble the bacon into tiny bits in the bowl. Stir in @walnuts{2%cups} until well combined and pour into the prepared pie crust.

To finish the pie, bake the pie until puffed and brown, until there is only a slight jiggle in the filling at the center of the pie when the rim of the plate is tapped, about ~{55%minutes}. Cool the pie on a wire rack for at least ~{90%minutes} or to room temperature before slicing into wedges to serve. Store tightly sealed with plastic wrap in the refrigerator for up to 2 days.

== Custard ==

Stir @heavy cream{1%cup}, @whole milk{1%cup}, @malted milk powder{2/3%cup}, and @pure vanilla extract{1/2%tsp} in a large saucepan set over medium heat until the powder has mostly dissolved and small bubbles form around the inside perimeter of the pan. Meanwhile, using an electric mixer, beat @egg yolks{4%large} (at room temperature), @white granulated sugar{1/2%cup}, and @?salt{1/4%tsp} in a large bowl until thick and pale yellow, until wide ribbons slide off the turned-off beaters, about ~{4%minutes}.

Beat about half the hot cream mixture into the egg mixture in a slow, steady stream until smooth, then beat this combined mixture into the remaining cream mixture in the pan. Set the pan over low heat and cook, stirring constantly, until the mixture thickly coats the back of a wooden spoon and the temperature registers 170°F, ~{4-7%minutes}.

Strain the mixture through a fine-mesh sieve into a bowl and refrigerate for at least ~{4%hours} or up to 2 days, covering once the mixture is cold.

== Freeze the Custard ==

Prepare an ice-cream maker. Stir the cold custard and freeze it in the machine according to the manufacturer’s instructions, until you can scoop up a small mound with edges that do not immediately start melting. Store in a sealed container in the freezer for up to 1 month.
